      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but isn't there a practical reality here? If a student's \"default\" mode of communication is inherently less structured or relies on different rules, it suggests a level of ingrained habit that might be harder to shift than just learning a new vocabulary. From an admissions perspective, if we see a heavy reliance on dialect, aren't we essentially looking at a marker for a student who hasn't yet mastered the cognitive control required for high-level formal logic and academic rigor? It feels like there's a correlation between the ability to switch to Standard English and the ability to think in the structured way our curriculum demands.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
